      "text": "Since $5^{10} = 9765625,$ $$5^{1993} = 555625 \\times 555625 \\times 555625.$$ Thus, the leftmost two digits are $55.$ The rightmost three digits of $5^{1993}$ are the same as the rightmost three digits of $\\underbrace{976}_{\\text{leftmost two digits of } 5^{10}}555$. Because $976<1000$,\\begin{align*}\n\\left(\\frac{976}{1000}\\right)\\underbrace{555}_{\\text{rightmost three digits of } \\underbrace{976\\ldots6}_{\\text{rightmost even three digits of } 5^{10}}555} &\\quad\\text{(note: no carry from this division)}\\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\underbrace{555}_{\\text{rightmost three digits of } \\underbrace{976\\ldots6}_{\\text{rightmost two digits of } 5^{10}}555}\\\\\n&= \\underline{Fine -- this is a weak generalization; the actual division is as follows:}\n\\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)55566\\\\\n&= \\underline{But scratch out and carry:}\\quad\\underline{976}55566\\nextstep\\\\\n&\\boxed{556}.\n\\end{align*}Note: There is a shortcut, of course: $5^{10} \\equiv 625 \\pmod{1001},$ so $5^{1993} \\equiv (5^{10})^{199} \\cdot 5^3 \\equiv (625)^{199} \\cdot 5^{3} \\equiv 625 \\cdot 5^3 \\equiv 625 \\cdot 125 \\equiv \\boxed{556}\\pmod{1001}.$Let's break down the solution process step-by-step to ensure clarity and reasonability.\n\nGiven:\n- We need to find the rightmost three digits of \\( 5^{1993} \\).\n- It is known that \\( 5^{10} = 9765625 \\).\n\nThus, we can express \\( 5^{1993} \\) in terms of \\( 5^{10} \\):\n\\[ 5^{1993} = 555625 \\times 555625 \\times 555625. \\]\n\nWe are interested only in the last three digits, which means we need to focus on the last three digits of \\( 5^{1993} \\).\n\nIf \\( a \\) numbers of \\( 5^{10} \\) are used, then the last three digits will be:\n\\[ (\\text{last three digits of } 5^{10})^{a \\text{ times}} = (9765625)^{a \\text{ times}}. \\]\n\nSo, we need to find \\( (9765625)^{a \\text{ times}} \\) and take the last three digits of that number. Specifically:\n\\[ 5^{1993} = 555625 \\times 555625 \\times 555625. \\]\n\nLet's decompose:\n\\[ 5^{1993} = (9765625)^{199} \\times (555625) \\times (555625). \\]\n\nFirstly, let's focus on \\( (9765625)^{199} \\). We break it down using modular arithmetic:\n\\[ (9765625)^{199} \\equiv 5^{199} \\pmod{1000}. \\]\n\\[ 5^{199} = (5^2)^{99} \\times 5^1 = 25^{99} \\times 5. \\]\n\nSince \\( 25 \\equiv 25 \\pmod{1000} \\), we have:\n\\[ 25^{99} \\equiv 5^1 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{1000}. \\]\n\nSo, \n\\[ (9765625)^{199} \\equiv 5 \\times 555625 \\times 555625 \\pmod{1000}. \\]\n\nSince only the last three digits matter:\n\\[ 5 \\times 555625 \\equiv 055625 \\pmod{1000}. \\]\n\nHence, \\( (9765625)^{199} \\mod 1000 = 556 \\).\n\nThe product of the last two digits of the expansion is:\n\\[ (555625) \\times (555625) \\]\n\nTo focus on the last three digits of this product, we continue using modular arithmetic:\n\\[ 555625 \\equiv 625 \\pmod{1000}. \\]\n\\[ 555625 \\times 555625 = 79088128125 \\]\n\\[ 79088128125 \\mod 1000 = 081 \\]\n\nThus, the rightmost three digits of \\( 5^{1993} \\) are the same as the rightmost three digits of \\( 081 \\).\n\nSo, the answer for the rightmost three digits of \\( 5^{1993} \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{081} \\]\n\nAfter discussing further, it could say:\n\nIn conclusion, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{081}\\).",
