      "text": "To find the rightmost three digits of $5^{1993}$, we need to find the value of $5^{1993} \\mod 1000$. \n\nThe number $5^{1993} = 126765060022822940149670320537_{} \\times 2^{1993}$. \n\nNow notice that the exponent is $1993$, so it will not cause a carry into the ones digit. In other words, $5^{1993}$ will be $5_{} \\times A$. This is because $5 \\lt 10$, their product will also have the ones digit $5$. Also notice that $1993 \\equiv 93 \\mod 100$. Therefore, $5^{1993} \\equiv 5 \\times 93^{100} \\mod 1000$. \n\nNext, notice that $93^{100} \\equiv 7^{100} \\mod 1000$. We know the $7^n$ digits are periodic in pairs: \n\n$\\begin{array}{c|cccccccc}n&1&2&3&4&5&6&7&8\\\\ &7&4&9&3&1&5&2&9\\end{array}$ \n\nThis has a period of $5$, so $(7^{100})^{100} \\equiv (7^4)^{25} \\equiv 42^2 \\equiv 97 \\mod 1000$. \n\nIndeed, we have $93^{100} - 1 \\equiv 93 \\times 2^{99} \\equiv 5^{1993} \\equiv 5 \\times 870 \\equiv 1267650600228224141743813189_{} \\times 93 \\equiv 5 \\times 93 = 465 \\mod 1000$. This is the ones digit of $5^{1993} \\ge 1$. \n\nFinally, let $2, 7, 256, 81$, and $15625$ denote the last three set of ones digits for $5^1, 5^2, 5^3, 5^4$, and $5^5$ as each $1000$ descending powers. \n\nFrom the computations above, we know that $5^{1993} \\equiv 12612804071_{} \\times 93 \\equiv 2292$",
